<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # My Family and I – Lesson # 4</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 4</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Writing</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 1st Grade (CEFR: Pre-A1.3)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> My Family and I</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> She Is My Mom!</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Writing: Productive: Write their own name and other very familiar words without assistance. Interactive: Draw pictures related to a simple sentence they have written.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Writing: Can write other very familiar family-related words without assistance when labeling a family drawing (e.g., "mom," "dad"). Can draw pictures related to simple sentences they have written about family members (e.g., drawing a picture of "my mom" to accompany the sentence, "This is my mom."). Can write familiar family words on cards, without assistance.</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 30%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 20%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher holds up large flashcards of family members (mom, dad, brother, sister). Teacher asks, "Who is this?" and students respond chorally, e.g., "Mom!" Teacher then focuses on the initial sound and letter. "Mom! Mmm... 'M' for Mom." Teacher writes a large 'M' on the board and models tracing it in the air. This prepares students for letter recognition and writing.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher displays the flashcard for "mom" and clearly writes the word "mom" on the whiteboard, enunciating each letter sound. "M-O-M, mom." The teacher then uses a finger to trace the word "mom" several times on the board, encouraging students to trace along in the air. This visually and kinesthetically models the writing action.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Pointing to the word 'mom' on the board) "Is this 'dad'?" (Expected answer: "No!")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Pointing to the word 'mom') "Is this 'mom'?" (Expected answer: "Yes!")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "My Family and I".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A large, illustrated "My Family" chart or big book with simple, repetitive sentences. Each page features a different family member (mom, dad, brother, sister) with a clear drawing and a short sentence like "This is my mom. She is friendly." or "This is my dad. He is tall." The text will clearly display the target grammar (Personal pronouns + verb, Possessive adjectives) and vocabulary.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Presentational Communication Competence: Learners communicate information, concepts, and ideas to an audience of listeners or readers on a variety of topics, specifically expressing information about family members through simple written forms.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> The teacher reads each sentence from the "My Family" chart/book aloud, pointing to the words as they read. After reading each sentence, the teacher asks students to point to the corresponding family member in the picture. For example, "This is my mom." (Teacher reads and points to text). "Where is my mom?" (Students point to the mom in the picture). This reinforces the connection between written words, images, and meaning, preparing them for writing their own sentences.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">7-10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Writing.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Writing familiar family words and simple sentences. Grammar: Personal pronouns + verb ("She is," "He is"), Possessive adjectives ("my mom," "my dad"). Vocabulary: mom, dad, brother, sister. Phonemic awareness: /m/ in "mom," /d/ in "dad," /b/ in "brother," /s/ in "sister."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: decimal; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice (Tracing Words):</b> Students receive a worksheet with clear images of "mom," "dad," "brother," and "sister." Below each picture, the corresponding word is written in large, dotted letters. Students use pencils to carefully trace each word multiple times, focusing on correct letter formation. The teacher circulates, providing individual guidance and encouraging careful tracing.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled Practice (Copying and Completing Sentences):</b> On the same worksheet, students find sections with partial sentences and blank lines or missing letters. For example: "This is my _______." or "This is my m__m." Students will first copy the complete words they just traced (e.g., "mom," "dad") into the blank spaces or fill in the missing letters. Then, they will copy the entire simple sentence (e.g., "This is my mom.") onto a line below, practicing independent word and sentence writing.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Interaction/Peer Check (Writing Recognition):</b> Students work in pairs. They exchange their completed worksheets. Partner A points to a word they have written (e.g., "mom") and asks, "What word is this?" Partner B identifies the word. They then switch roles. This activity encourages legibility and recognition of the written family words.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "My Family and I".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Individual creative writing and drawing activity.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Description:</b> Students receive a blank sheet of paper divided into two sections. In the first section, the teacher instructs them: "Draw your mom here." After students complete their drawing, the teacher says, "Now, below your drawing, write the sentence: 'This is my mom.'" The teacher writes the sentence clearly on the board as a model, but encourages students to write independently if possible. In the second section, the teacher instructs, "Draw your dad here." After drawing, "Write the sentence: 'This is my dad.' below your drawing." Students focus on accurately writing the words "mom" and "dad" and forming the simple sentence, connecting their drawings to their written descriptions. This activity directly addresses the learning outcome of writing familiar family words and drawing related pictures.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative assessment strategy:</b> Teacher observation during the production stage, focusing on individual student effort and accuracy in writing. A simple checklist can be used to note which students can write the target words independently versus those still needing support. Review of student's completed written work.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students submit their "My Family: Drawing and Writing" sheet (from Stage 4). The teacher collects and reviews each student's work to assess their ability to write familiar family words and simple sentences and to draw related pictures.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Writes "mom" and "dad" without assistance (words are recognizable).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Writes their own name (if included in the task) legibly.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Drawings clearly relate to the accompanying written sentences (e.g., a drawing of a mom for "This is my mom.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Attempts to form simple sentences using "This is my..." correctly.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students are given a small paper slip with two simple questions to answer by circling 'Yes' or 'No' or drawing a happy/sad face:</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can I write 'mom' and 'dad'?" (Yes / No)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Did I draw my family?" (Yes / No)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new words did you write today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How did you feel about writing sentences about your family?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> What worked well in terms of engaging students in writing activities? Were the writing tasks appropriately challenging for 1st graders (Pre-A1.3)? What specific challenges did students face with letter formation or word recognition during writing? How can I provide additional scaffolds, such as more tracing practice or letter formation guides, for students who struggled with independent writing in the next lesson? Did the integration of phonemic awareness aid their writing?</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> "Great job writing about your family today! Next lesson, we will use the words we've learned to create special family cards and share them with our classmates, telling them about 'my mom' and 'my dad'!" (This transitions to Lesson 5: Mediation & Integration, involving consolidating written output for a communicative purpose).</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">3-5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>